the class environment here encourages and

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.750
appreciates active participation
from all students.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:25.180
So, I think coming from
a background where in a classroom

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:29.440
environment you're not used to sharing
those many experiences or views.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.287
It was important to appreciate that is
the culture that wants you to share

00:05:34.287 --> 00:05:36.998
your views and experience in the class.

00:05:36.998 --> 00:05:41.150
&gt;&gt; Yeah, very open and honest feedback
you'll get, whether you want it or

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:41.978
not, right?

00:05:41.978 --> 00:05:49.518
[LAUGH] Are there specific admissions
requirements for international students?

00:05:49.518 --> 00:05:55.210
I'll add my take to this,
and that is, explicitly, no.

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:58.810
One of the requirements that we do
ask of international candidates,

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:04.160
if you have attended
a university where the primary

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:09.550
language of instruction was not
in English, we do require the.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:14.530
We evaluate all candidates based on
the same three criteria as I discussed in

00:06:14.530 --> 00:06:19.430
the video, intellectual vitality,
demonstrated leadership potential,

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.760
and personal qualities and contributions.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.460
So all candidates are evaluated, equally.

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:30.875
The next question, what is life like
at the GSB as an international student?

00:06:33.312 --> 00:06:38.210
&gt;&gt; I think GSB is an environment where
you have a lot of diversity in as many

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:40.210
aspects as you can think of.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:41.970
And as international students,

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:46.420
I'm in a class with international
students from more than 60 countries.

00:06:46.420 --> 00:06:50.740
And, I think, everybody comes
up with their own background,

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:52.520
own way in which they grew up.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:53.470
And it's.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:56.500
I have found it, personally, very
fascinating to just appreciate the fact

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:03.060
that how different people here look at
life or what motivates them, personally.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:08.230
I think, for
some of us who have not grown up in

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:13.190
a culture with a lot diversity,
it's really good to appreciate how

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:17.612
people think about different things,
both in personal life and academically.

00:07:17.612 --> 00:07:21.460
And [INAUDIBLE] I really
appreciated the American way of

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.620
looking at both academic and
non-academic things.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:28.060
And how or what students from other
countries bring to the table.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.450
&gt;&gt; For you, anything?

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:36.940
&gt;&gt; I think, I'm really impressed by
the kind of resources that exist for

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:38.230
international students.

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:41.280
I'm an international student that
has never lived in the US before.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.860
I've lived all my life in
India before moving here.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:46.600
But I found so much support all around me.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:50.310
Because, like I mentioned in my
previous answer, there are so

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:54.970
many resources available to socialize
the way you want to socialize.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:56.870
Mingling with other like minded people.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:59.950
I currently work in the Students'
Association International Committee.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:05.980
We put in a lot of thought to actually
incorporate all of the major international

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:07.150
events in Stanford life.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:09.980
We recently, both of us together,
organized a Diwali party,

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:12.930
which was a big hit,
which was attended by almost.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:18.270
People from all the other
nationalities as well.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:22.910
Similarly, there is so much happening
with different identity clubs that

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:26.720
are there at GSP with
Latin America Student Association.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.360
There's a Business of India
club which [INAUDIBLE] heads.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.530
There's an Africa Business Club, there is
a Hispanic Students Business Association.

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:36.180
So there are so many places where

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:41.090
you get a lot of support
career-wise as well as socially.

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:45.330
So that's a great thing at JSB for
international students according to me.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:46.100
&gt;&gt; Okay, great.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:47.130
Thank you.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:52.375
The next question I'll address as well,
which is, how do I craft a good essay?

00:08:52.375 --> 00:08:55.390
[LAUGH] It's a popular question.

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:59.580
What I will say,
as you think about writing your essay,

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.260
it's important that you
use your authentic voice.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:10.090
First and foremost what we're trying
to identify when we read your essay

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:14.410
is not only what matters most to you,
but explicitly why.

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:19.080
We want to understand your core values,

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:24.110
we want to hear from your perspectives,
your inner voice, what's important to you.

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:29.290
And I would say that it's not
necessarily a crafting of your essay

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:33.100
as much as it is being authentic and
true to yourself and

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.410
being able to convey that in
a clear manner in the essay.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:39.150
You guys have anything to add there?

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:45.460
&gt;&gt; I think, I would just like to
reiterate, it's not a place you just

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:49.760
show your strength, and I would encourage
all of you to be authentic, and

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:53.990
these are some things that the GSB
community prides itself in.

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:57.560
So it's a good way to show
that through your essays.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:02.240
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would also like to add
that it's not an individual exercise.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:03.870
I think it's a team effort.

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:06.620
I personally ran my essay by,

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:11.550
like, my closest friends and
colleagues and family, some of them.

00:10:11.550 --> 00:10:15.230
So it was really useful to take input
of people who know you very well.

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:16.120
&gt;&gt; Yes, yeah.

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:18.340
&gt;&gt; To add a lot of color to your essay.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:21.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
we often will say it is helpful to get

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:25.120
perspective from those who
know you incredibly well, and

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.830
when they read your essay,
that it sounds like you and only you.

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:29.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:36.550
&gt;&gt; The next question is how
did you finance your MBA?

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:40.510
Is it affordable for
the average Indian person?

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:44.050
And then I'll address some of
the questions regarding financial aid and

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:44.580
scholarships.

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:51.120
&gt;&gt; So I personally used a mix
of three different sources.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.690
And a small part of it was
my own personal savings from

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:57.130
the job I did before business school.

00:10:57.130 --> 00:11:00.820
The second major part was the financial
aid that I received from the school, and

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:03.580
the third part was the student loan that

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:06.280
is available to all students
that apply to the GSP.

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:10.880
So I think these are the three sources
that helped me finance my MBA and

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:12.860
to the cogent of it being affordable.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:17.490
I come from a middle-class family, and
I think these three sources combine, like,

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:23.320
put you in a comfortable
position to do this MBA.

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:28.000
&gt;&gt; In my case, I am a recipient
of Reliance Dhirubhi Fellowship

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.340
which covered a majority of my expenses.

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:31.723
Apart from that,

00:11:31.723 --> 00:11:37.630
I also used some of my savings from before
GSP as well as some family support.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:38.533
&gt;&gt; Okay yeah.

00:11:38.533 --> 00:11:43.794
Yes we do offer
a Reliance Dhirubhai Fellowship for

00:11:43.794 --> 00:11:52.010
Indian citizens who can benefit from
two years of nearly full-time tuition.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:57.380
So that scholarship, that fellowship
is available to all candidates.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:00.580
Who are citizens of India.

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:04.990
Did you consider any graduate
programs in India and

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:10.956
why did you decide to come to the US for
your MBA?

00:12:10.956 --> 00:12:15.860
&gt;&gt; I actually did not consider
other MBA programs in India,

00:12:15.860 --> 00:12:20.670
I really liked the job, at which I
got through an internship that I did

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:24.566
it in my third year
while I was in college.

00:12:24.566 --> 00:12:29.160
I wanted to walk before I do my MBA and

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:32.980
I was particularly fascinated
by an MBA in the US

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:37.820
when I saw some of my seniors in college
that I really look up to go that way.

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:42.600
And hearing from their experiences about
doing an MBA from a school like Stanford,

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.890
I understood the global perspective and

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:51.049
the kind of diverse experience we
get in an MBA school in the US.

00:12:52.122 --> 00:12:55.940
And that to, having worked for a couple
of years before doing an MBA is really

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:00.450
valuable and it's very different
from doing an MBA in India.

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:04.459
I have a lot fo friends who
have done MBA in India and

00:13:04.459 --> 00:13:08.948
they all have great careers I
have nothing against it but

00:13:08.948 --> 00:13:15.269
I just feel like a US MBA is pretty
diverse, and it's a great life experience.

00:13:15.269 --> 00:13:16.181
&gt;&gt; Okay, great.

00:13:16.181 --> 00:13:18.523
&gt;&gt; I think in addition
to global perspective,

00:13:18.523 --> 00:13:21.720
I think it also encourages
you to think of the cohort.

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:25.890
The cohort here, as I said, comes from
more than 60 different countries,

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:29.640
have varying level of work experience.

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.752
And I think that sort of diversity's
very difficult to replicate in an Indian

00:13:33.752 --> 00:13:35.180
graduate school.

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.977
So I think that is one factor
that should help for me.

00:13:37.977 --> 00:13:39.920
&gt;&gt; Okay, okay.

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:42.320
Moving along,
we'll go to the next question.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:44.860
Can you talk about some
of your favorite classes?

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:45.763
You probably have one to share about.

00:13:45.763 --> 00:13:49.645
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And professors?

00:13:49.645 --> 00:13:54.970
&gt;&gt; Sure, so I am just rushing
from a touchy feeling class.

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:57.830
I've just concluded my touchy
feely course this quarter.

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:02.588
Which was a life changing experience,
at the cost of sounding cliched,

00:14:02.588 --> 00:14:06.930
but it's undoubtedly the best
experience at GSB so far.

00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:13.880
Second, there are a lot of other great
classes, and particularly in Western

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:17.790
technology, so I do a lot of courses
in technology and entrepreneurship.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:19.840
So there is a great course
called Electronic Business,

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:22.940
which is in the first year, then there's
another great course of Building and

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:26.040
Managing Seamless Organization,
which is a very practical course.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:33.050
And you get to listen to some of the
prominent sales leaders in the industry.

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:35.680
There are several other courses for

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:38.470
people interested in entrepreneurship
like Startup Garage,

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:44.660
where you can start from the scratch,
from idea phase to execution phase.

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:46.762
A lot of people who are into
entrepreneurship really liked the class.

00:14:46.762 --> 00:14:47.909
Yeah.

00:14:47.909 --> 00:14:49.631
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:14:49.631 --> 00:14:54.097
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think for me the classes
which are related to communication or

00:14:54.097 --> 00:14:57.450
matching difficult
conversations have stood out.

00:14:57.450 --> 00:15:01.330
And one class that comes to mind
is managing growing enterprises by

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:04.640
Joel Peterson who is
the chairman of Jet Blue.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:08.530
And that class basically takes about how
do you have difficult conversations with

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:09.400
the board.

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:11.020
How do you fire someone?

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:14.940
How do you give feedback to an employee
who has not been performing well?

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:20.988
So, some of these conversations we
will face at all phases of our life.

00:15:20.988 --> 00:15:24.250
and just practicing them in our risk-free
environment is something that I

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:25.430
valued a lot.

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:26.160
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:30.480
Yeah I find oftentimes I talk to students
also about, or perspective candidates

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:34.170
about leadership labs which addresses
a lot of those same questions.

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:35.960
What do you do if you
have to fire someone?

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:37.170
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; How do you handle difficult

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:37.780
conversations?

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:41.420
So you'll get lots of practice on that.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:43.900
The next question is what percent of your

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:47.840
coursework is theory
versus hands-on learning?

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:53.180
Do you guys want to share a little bit
about what your perspective has been, or

00:15:53.180 --> 00:15:55.410
experience in the classroom
as far as that goes?

00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:59.870
I think it would be hard to
put a percentage value to it.

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:04.880
But I would say most of
the learning here is experiential,

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:10.250
and sort of working in groups or
teams to basically work that through.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:14.826
For example, even in the first quarter,
we start with something called lead laps,

00:16:14.826 --> 00:16:19.273
in which you have a cohort of six students
who basically give feedback to each other

00:16:19.273 --> 00:16:22.378
every week on what about you
encourages me to follow you?

00:16:22.378 --> 00:16:23.428
It's a very simple question.

00:16:23.428 --> 00:16:28.301
But just the fact that you hear how
your actions land on different people in

00:16:28.301 --> 00:16:31.207
a small cohort week
after week just sort of,

00:16:31.207 --> 00:16:34.360
it's a good lab to put yourself through.

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:38.240
I think some of these activities where
you are either interacting with different

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:42.020
people or
doing it hands-on on a software or

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:47.390
on a lab just makes the learning process
much more interactive and much more fun.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:52.660
So I would say most of my learning has
been experiential of working in teams.

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:56.556
Having said that, some of the courses
which require critical work,

00:16:56.556 --> 00:16:59.117
like accounting and stuff, would be there.

00:16:59.117 --> 00:17:01.990
And I think that's the best way
to learn some of those courses.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:02.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:02.860 --> 00:17:05.508
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, so it's a mix of both.

00:17:05.508 --> 00:17:09.117
There is a good number of courses
which are heavily theoretical.

00:17:09.117 --> 00:17:14.864
And there's equal number of courses which
are experiential and practical in nature.

00:17:14.864 --> 00:17:18.660
At the risk of sounding repetitive,
touchy, feely is very practical.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:21.162
There's little to no
theory involved in it.

00:17:21.162 --> 00:17:24.548
And similarly,
is like one out of a class, but

00:17:24.548 --> 00:17:29.030
four to five hours of practical
work outside of class every week.

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:32.758
There are a lot of design school courses
which are very popular amongst the GSB

00:17:32.758 --> 00:17:34.604
students, which are all hands-on.

00:17:34.604 --> 00:17:37.575
There are a few courses
which involve fieldwork

00:17:37.575 --> 00:17:42.487
that are definitely global experience
problems which count as a coursework.

00:17:42.487 --> 00:17:43.896
But it is like, basically,

00:17:43.896 --> 00:17:47.440
going to a different country
with a bunch of your classmates.

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:48.060
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:49.950
it's an interesting mix of both,
I would say.

00:17:51.430 --> 00:17:52.570
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:17:52.570 --> 00:17:55.505
The next question we'll take is,
what clubs and

00:17:55.505 --> 00:17:58.521
activities outside of
class are you enrolled in?

00:18:00.906 --> 00:18:01.474
&gt;&gt; I'll go.

00:18:01.474 --> 00:18:02.397
&gt;&gt; When you have free time.

00:18:02.397 --> 00:18:08.280
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I'm involved in a couple of things.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:11.190
I am part of Students Association.

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:13.770
I work in career committee and
international committee,

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:18.610
because these are the two things
which are kind of dear to me.

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:22.460
I am really passionate about
ensuring international students

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:26.150
get all the required resources to pursue
the careers that they wanted to pursue.

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:28.764
And like I mentioned,

00:18:28.764 --> 00:18:33.170
I also came to GSB with a goal of
transition into tech product management.

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:36.990
So I got involved in
Tech Club very earlier on.

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:38.630
I believe there's a [INAUDIBLE] Tech Club.

00:18:40.787 --> 00:18:46.430
Yeah, I also sit on the senate where
a lot of important discussions and

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:48.830
decisions are made.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:54.940
So I would say all these experiences
have enriched by GSB life so far.

00:18:56.620 --> 00:18:59.475
&gt;&gt; And you can keep busy, for sure.

00:18:59.475 --> 00:19:01.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for me, it's very similar.

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:04.450
I co-chair the academic committee here and

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:08.600
also sit on the senate of
the student association with.

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:12.870
I am deeply interested in
businesses in India, and

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:16.310
I'm the President for
the Business in India club here.

00:19:16.310 --> 00:19:19.691
In addition, I'm also involved
in a committee called Future of

00:19:19.691 --> 00:19:23.090
Management Education,
in which we are holistically looking at

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:27.420
what are the different ways in which we
can basically cater to the MBA program

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:30.420
here and make it even more attractive for
students going forward.

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:31.370
&gt;&gt; Okay, great.

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:39.810
The next question, how has your proximity
to Silicon Valley benefitted you compared

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:45.550
to other programs not in the Bay Area?

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:50.550
&gt;&gt; I think the biggest benefit for
me is the proximity

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:54.750
to all the protagonists of the cases
that we do in the classes.

00:19:54.750 --> 00:20:00.960
All the classes which cover technology and
entrepreneurship have

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:06.460
cases surrounding real life
protagonists who live right

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:09.410
outside Stanford,
like in Palo Alto and Mountain View.

00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:11.580
So it's very easy to have
access to people like that.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:15.770
So every single day,
in each of my classes, I have one of these

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:20.050
people who have taken a company public or
have achieved several other great things

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:23.980
in the technology space,
venture capitalists come and talk to us,

00:20:23.980 --> 00:20:28.860
which is something I can't imagine
happening as easily in other schools.

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:33.710
Secondly, being one of
the leaders of the Tech Club,

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:38.840
I can totally see how it benefits us
in the way of networking as well.

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:43.670
It’s so easy for us to call anybody out
and ask them to come out and do a BBL,

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:47.390
which is a brown bag lunch or lunchtime
session, where they come and talk about

00:20:47.390 --> 00:20:52.190
topic of their interest or just represent
the company that wants to hire from GSB.

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:56.610
So I think these are the two benefits
which I can very obviously see how

00:20:56.610 --> 00:20:58.340
it benefits Stanford or any other school.

00:20:59.730 --> 00:21:04.370
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and just in terms of access
to protagonists on the cases,

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:07.200
it's also proximity to
the culture of Silicon Valley.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:11.290
I think this is a culture which
sort of celebrates taking risks,

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:15.200
being entrepreneurial,
going after what seems impossible.

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:19.220
And just being in that environment sort
of, especially for risk-averse people like

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:23.800
me, sort of encourages you to go think
outside the box, go achieve your dream.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:28.020
And so I think it tells us a good
mental lab to put yourself through.

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:30.880
&gt;&gt; Good, great, all right.

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:32.817
What was the application and

00:21:32.817 --> 00:21:37.018
admissions process like from
your perspective as a student?

00:21:41.139 --> 00:21:45.157
&gt;&gt; I think, for me,
the application process was, in a way,

00:21:45.157 --> 00:21:47.701
great mental journey to go through.

00:21:47.701 --> 00:21:52.191
Because the application process sort of
encouraged me to think deeply about what

00:21:52.191 --> 00:21:54.810
matters to me and
what defines me as a person.

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:59.560
And I think this a culture which
is like a vastly celebrated, or

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:01.640
a GSB of knowing yourself well.

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:04.290
And the application
process sort of encourages

00:22:04.290 --> 00:22:06.720
each one of us to sort of go
through that mental journey.

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:13.030
And it was also interesting to get
a chance to interview with an alumni and

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:19.280
share their experiences,
what they valued at the GSB.

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:21.420
So I think it's a good mix
of getting to know yourself,

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:24.320
getting to know GSB from
different diverse perspectives.

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:27.106
So it was a good reflection of
the GSB culture in general.

00:22:27.106 --> 00:22:30.699
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we talk about
the journey here at the GSB, but

00:22:30.699 --> 00:22:34.384
it really starts in
the application process, yeah.

00:22:34.384 --> 00:22:36.700
Do you have anything you
want to add to that?

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:42.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I strongly agree with what
said about it being a mental journey.

00:22:42.190 --> 00:22:43.830
It's a deep personal experience.

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:47.620
I don't think any other school's
application essay would probe you to think

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:50.920
as much as yourself as much
as the Stanford essay does.

00:22:50.920 --> 00:22:56.480
When I started writing my response
to what matters to most to

00:22:56.480 --> 00:23:01.710
you and why, it really prompted me
to think about my entire life and

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:03.650
pick out instances that
really matter to me.

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:09.256
And that journey really extended when
I came to GSB, like I gave a talk.

00:23:09.256 --> 00:23:10.613
It was like an extension of that.

00:23:10.613 --> 00:23:14.219
So it’s really interesting how
the journey which started,

00:23:14.219 --> 00:23:17.825
what matters to you most and
why, keeps getting iterated and

00:23:17.825 --> 00:23:21.304
improved upon through several
other experiences at GSP.

00:23:21.304 --> 00:23:24.991
&gt;&gt; Yeah, a deeply introspective
process that continues, even here.

00:23:24.991 --> 00:23:25.565
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:25.565 --> 00:23:30.618
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How did you select the person

00:23:30.618 --> 00:23:35.220
who wrote your recommendation letter?

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:37.590
&gt;&gt; I think it was kind of obvious for me.

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:43.020
Because I selected my immediate manager,
and his manager,

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:47.290
the two people who had the best view to
my work, because that was my first job.

00:23:47.290 --> 00:23:50.720
I haven't really spent
time in any other company.

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:56.330
I joined Unilever right out of college and
spent all my time there.

00:23:56.330 --> 00:24:01.890
And my first boss saw me go
through being a first time

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:07.710
manager to finishing off
a stint of two years properly.

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:11.190
So he was the best person,
position to write about me.

00:24:11.190 --> 00:24:15.270
And similarly, his manager also had
good view to what happened through

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:20.050
my career at Unilever, so these are the
two people who were my obvious choices.

00:24:20.050 --> 00:24:24.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'll go to you too, but
I want to add to what you said,

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:27.130
which another question is who
should applicants choose.

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:31.625
And I think it's important that as
you are considering individuals,

00:24:31.625 --> 00:24:35.218
that you do look to those who
know you most well, right?

00:24:35.218 --> 00:24:38.728
So those who have had
the closest experience and

00:24:38.728 --> 00:24:44.884
can speak specifically to things that
you have done, what you've accomplished.

00:24:44.884 --> 00:24:49.944
And so it's more important that you
find someone who knows you well and

00:24:49.944 --> 00:24:51.847
can write on that behalf.

00:24:51.847 --> 00:24:53.430
What do you want to share?

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:55.900
&gt;&gt; I think you covered it mostly.

00:24:55.900 --> 00:24:58.380
I would just encourage you to take

00:24:58.380 --> 00:25:02.480
the recommendations of someone who knows
you well and can talk specific examples.

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:06.052
Because that is something that
the admission officer appreciates.

00:25:06.052 --> 00:25:10.640
Some tangible examples of
the qualities they are highlighting in

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:12.230
the recommendation letter.

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:14.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
we do have requirements, of course,

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:19.020
that you would want to select your
immediate direct current supervisor,

00:25:19.020 --> 00:25:22.420
unless there is a reason that
you are unable to do so.

00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:27.748
What is student life like at the GSB and

00:25:27.748 --> 00:25:32.035
is it easy to blend in with the people?

00:25:32.035 --> 00:25:37.305
&gt;&gt; I think GSB people are some of
the easiest people to blend in with

00:25:37.305 --> 00:25:43.878
because everybody comes in with an aim to
get to know other people in the class.

00:25:43.878 --> 00:25:49.283
And because we all care deeply about
introspection and self-awareness,

00:25:49.283 --> 00:25:54.431
people are very grounded and
people really care about knowing where you

00:25:54.431 --> 00:26:00.140
come from than sort of just at
the superficial details of your resume.

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:02.960
So I think there are a lot
of opportunities to

00:26:02.960 --> 00:26:04.270
meet a lot of interesting people.

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:08.970
I felt at home right away.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:09.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:26:10.830 --> 00:26:11.910
&gt;&gt; I think very similar.

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:14.890
I think this is a culture that sort
of celebrates the differences and

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:17.700
sort of encourages you to be yourself.

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:22.510
And I think one common phrase that we use
here is, fitting in and standing out.

00:26:22.510 --> 00:26:25.320
And I think both are equally
celebrated here.

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:28.130
There are a lot of things
that we share as a class.

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:30.410
And in that sense, we fit in.

00:26:30.410 --> 00:26:31.910
But we also celebrate standing out,

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:35.500
because each one of us bring
a different thing to the class.

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:38.520
And that is something that we
as a community appreciate,

00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:40.080
want to develop further.

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:45.948
So it's not a pressure to just fit in, but
also stand out in your own unique way.

00:26:45.948 --> 00:26:48.270
&gt;&gt; Very good, great.

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:53.802
What should one focus on when
they start attending Stanford?

00:26:53.802 --> 00:26:57.920
So preparation-wise, so to speak,
how do you decide what classes and

00:26:57.920 --> 00:27:01.130
experiences to take advantage of?

00:27:01.130 --> 00:27:02.930
&gt;&gt; So I can start on this one.

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:07.480
It sort of goes back to the point that
it's really important that you have two or

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:11.550
three broad goals for
yourself when you come to Stanford.

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:14.910
When you come here, there will be
literally millions of opportunities and

00:27:14.910 --> 00:27:17.260
you would want to be a part of dance show,

00:27:17.260 --> 00:27:21.280
you would want to be part of this club and
this certain activity.

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:26.261
For me, in the first week when I came
to Stanford, I made a list of like,

00:27:26.261 --> 00:27:30.597
these are the three things I want
to walk away out of Stanford.

00:27:30.597 --> 00:27:33.263
And every activity I
sort of try to do here,

00:27:33.263 --> 00:27:37.572
I use this lens to evaluate whether
it makes sense for me personally.

00:27:37.572 --> 00:27:41.285
And it is just good to keep a center and
be honest to yourself, so

00:27:41.285 --> 00:27:46.400
that when you are done with the two years,
you feel proud of what you have done here.

00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:47.580
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that should be done.

00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:50.843
I often hear people say it's like
drinking out of a water hose.

00:27:50.843 --> 00:27:53.397
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There's so many options and

00:27:53.397 --> 00:27:55.600
opportunties to get involved.

00:27:55.600 --> 00:28:00.250
It is helpful to sort of have
a little bit of an idea or

00:28:00.250 --> 00:28:03.780
a focus but also be open to other options.

00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:04.280
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:28:05.670 --> 00:28:06.450
&gt;&gt; Plus one.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:07.600
&gt;&gt; Okay, all right.

00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:11.690
Have you taken any classes
outside of the GSB?

00:28:13.070 --> 00:28:14.950
&gt;&gt; I did a couple in my first year.

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:20.130
I did a couple of class in
the computer science department, and

00:28:20.130 --> 00:28:24.440
I also did a really extensive
design school class.

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:29.002
Both were very unique experiences,
nothing like GSB.

00:28:29.002 --> 00:28:32.439
A lot of fun getting to
know people outside of GSB.

00:28:32.439 --> 00:28:34.720
It's called across-the-street classes, and

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:38.203
it's a great way to get out of the GSB
bubble, especially in the first year.

00:28:38.203 --> 00:28:41.112
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Where you live in a dorm right across

00:28:41.112 --> 00:28:45.879
your classroom complex, so
it's one way to really get out there and

00:28:45.879 --> 00:28:50.670
meet other interesting people in
the broader Stanford community.

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:51.815
&gt;&gt; Taking classes,

00:28:51.815 --> 00:28:56.483
I'll say GSB is like a very unique
value proposition that Stanford has.

00:28:56.483 --> 00:29:00.130
Just the proximity and the relationship
GSB shares with other school

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:04.900
within the Stanford family, just gives
you a lot of different perspectives and

00:29:04.900 --> 00:29:09.050
helps you basically your
network beyond GSB.

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:12.570
I took a really interesting class,
Public Speaking Across the Street,

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:16.540
which was very unique and very different
from the classes we do at the GSB,

00:29:16.540 --> 00:29:20.780
so it's a great resource that
is available to GSB students.

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:22.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
they're talking about across the street,

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:27.680
which of course is our term that
we use for really any of the other

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:31.420
leading professional graduate schools
on campus that you'll have access to.

00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:34.110
You can literally walk within 10 or
15 minutes and

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:39.210
be at some of these other
professional schools on campus.

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:45.300
And it is why over 90% of our students
will take a course across the street.

00:29:45.300 --> 00:29:46.790
So it's a big part of your experience.

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:52.690
What is the professional background
of your peers at the GSB?

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:55.410
Are they diverse in industry and
experience?

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:59.570
I think we touched a little bit on the
importance of diversity within the class.

00:29:59.570 --> 00:30:00.590
But anything to elaborate on?

00:30:02.070 --> 00:30:06.840
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think in terms of professional
diversity, this is as wide as it gets.

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:12.700
There are people from consulting, private
equity, to military, to operations.

00:30:12.700 --> 00:30:16.861
I have one of my classmates
who was a NASCAR racedriver.

00:30:16.861 --> 00:30:20.786
So it's like diversity in
terms of as broad as it gets.

00:30:20.786 --> 00:30:24.483
And the way it sort of shows up and
is helpful in the classroom is your

00:30:24.483 --> 00:30:29.108
professional background shapes the way you
look at different business problems or

00:30:29.108 --> 00:30:30.650
non-business problems.

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:34.730
And just having that diversity encourages
you to think in many different ways

00:30:34.730 --> 00:30:38.190
to a problem which you wouldn't
have thought of otherwise.

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:42.110
So I think it's as diverse
as it can politically get.

00:30:42.110 --> 00:30:43.871
&gt;&gt; Yeah, totally agree with that.

00:30:43.871 --> 00:30:47.104
And not to mention the number of
Colombians we have in our class.

00:30:47.104 --> 00:30:51.753
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think this diversity's really

00:30:51.753 --> 00:30:56.079
helpful in helping you with your
own career pursuits, I think.

00:30:56.079 --> 00:31:00.288
Because if you want to make a switch from
one industry to a different industry,

00:31:00.288 --> 00:31:04.124
there are already a few people who
have done that before coming to GSB.

00:31:04.124 --> 00:31:07.627
So you can just grab a coffee with them
and understand what that industry's all

00:31:07.627 --> 00:31:10.034
about or what that particular
function is all about.

00:31:10.034 --> 00:31:10.721
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:31:10.721 --> 00:31:15.600
&gt;&gt; And get some key contacts which will
help you make inroads into that new career

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:17.750
that you want to pursue.

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:19.860
And people are so
willing to help here all the time,

00:31:21.210 --> 00:31:24.480
that everybody is just a phone call or
a text message away.

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:29.830
So this diversity can really be
leveraged to follow your passions.

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:30.910
&gt;&gt; Great, thank you.

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:36.630
When is the right time to apply to
the GSB and which round is best?

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:41.060
So I talked a little bit about the right
time to apply is when you are ready.

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:44.960
That could mean right when
you graduate from college.

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:49.700
It could mean that you want two, three,
five, ten years of working experience.

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:52.570
From our perspective, and
in the admissions office,

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:57.390
when you decide to apply is Not
necessarily important to us,

00:31:57.390 --> 00:32:01.310
but it's important for
us to understand why it is now.

00:32:01.310 --> 00:32:08.870
One of the other questions was
about which round is fast,

00:32:08.870 --> 00:32:15.490
in general it is preferred to
apply in round one or round two,

00:32:15.490 --> 00:32:20.430
there are certain opportunities
that we provide as a result.

00:32:20.430 --> 00:32:24.160
If you are a round one or round two
applicant we offer admit weekend, so

00:32:24.160 --> 00:32:28.460
you have the opportunity to come
here on campus if at all possible,

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:32.410
there are other access that
you'll have available to you.

00:32:32.410 --> 00:32:35.670
But we certainly want you to
apply when you're ready, so

00:32:35.670 --> 00:32:39.060
if that means waiting until
round three that's fine as well.

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:42.050
Not sure if you guys have any perspective,

00:32:42.050 --> 00:32:44.900
having gone through the application
yourself, the process.

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:47.690
&gt;&gt; I think you got it well, and

00:32:47.690 --> 00:32:53.110
I would encourage you to not worry too
much about round one versus round two.

00:32:53.110 --> 00:32:54.893
Round three is something that, as I said,

00:32:54.893 --> 00:32:57.182
should only be used in
extraordinary circumstances.

00:32:57.182 --> 00:33:01.707
But between round one and round two,
I would not put a lot of maths behind it,

00:33:01.707 --> 00:33:05.174
whenever you are ready and
you think it's the best time and

00:33:05.174 --> 00:33:06.907
what round's best for you.

00:33:06.907 --> 00:33:13.350
&gt;&gt; Okay, all right, what is the Reliance
Fellowship and what are the requirements?

00:33:13.350 --> 00:33:18.380
So I touched on this a little bit,
which is that we do offer a fellowship for

00:33:18.380 --> 00:33:21.710
those individuals who live in India and

00:33:21.710 --> 00:33:26.590
have a desire to return to
India after graduating.

00:33:27.750 --> 00:33:31.960
There are specific requirements,
you can go online to our website and

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:36.510
learn more about that under
our fellowship opportunities.

00:33:36.510 --> 00:33:39.220
Do you have any classmates
who you happen to know, or

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:42.110
of course do you want to speak to?

00:33:42.110 --> 00:33:46.810
&gt;&gt; For sure, so the process to apply for
this fellowship is to

00:33:46.810 --> 00:33:52.390
actually it begins before any of
the GSB application process starts.

00:33:52.390 --> 00:33:57.388
It starts with an essay that you have to
write about what are your aspirations for

00:33:57.388 --> 00:34:01.358
India, how do you want to see
India shape up in the near future?

00:34:01.358 --> 00:34:05.043
Based on that essay and
your resume you will be short-listed,

00:34:05.043 --> 00:34:07.627
a bunch of 50 students is short-listed?

00:34:07.627 --> 00:34:09.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah, 50 finalists.

00:34:09.700 --> 00:34:13.720
&gt;&gt; And there is a networking session which
is organized towards the end of August,

00:34:13.720 --> 00:34:18.410
I think, where you'll meet with
several Stanford admissions or staff,

00:34:18.410 --> 00:34:21.630
as well as several alumni based in India.

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:24.120
There are a couple of group activities and

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:27.130
a lot of interaction with Stanford
alumni and fellow applicants.

00:34:28.970 --> 00:34:33.720
And once you do that, you apply along
with everybody else in round one or

00:34:33.720 --> 00:34:34.530
round two, depend.

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:38.480
When I applied to it I could only apply
in round one, but it has been flexed

00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:40.950
last year and now you can apply
either in round one or round two.

00:34:42.660 --> 00:34:46.360
Normal application process there's nothing
different about the Stanford application

00:34:46.360 --> 00:34:50.640
process, the only additional step is
the essay which you have to submit

00:34:50.640 --> 00:34:54.730
around mid-July and the networking
session that I just talked about.

00:34:54.730 --> 00:34:58.679
And these 50 finalists will apply like any
other regular Stanford applicant in either

00:34:58.679 --> 00:34:59.757
round one or round two.

00:34:59.757 --> 00:35:03.689
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and so is talking about
the pre-application process,

00:35:03.689 --> 00:35:06.713
which is what you would
do in the early summer.

00:35:06.713 --> 00:35:11.551
And then we also offer a Reliance
finalists day where we bring in everyone

00:35:11.551 --> 00:35:16.548
to learn a little bit more about the MBA
program and the GSB experience, and

00:35:16.548 --> 00:35:21.330
give you the opportunity to meet
alumni of the program as well.

00:35:21.330 --> 00:35:25.720
And then the second part of
the process is for you to apply,

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:30.660
just like any other candidate, through
the general MBA application process and

00:35:30.660 --> 00:35:32.530
we'd evaluate you as such.

00:35:32.530 --> 00:35:33.380
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:37.660
&gt;&gt; What career networking and
other professional opportunities

00:35:37.660 --> 00:35:41.440
have you been able to take
advantage of while at the GSB?

00:35:43.930 --> 00:35:46.850
&gt;&gt; So
I think in terms of career opportunities,

00:35:46.850 --> 00:35:49.490
this is an ocean of opportunities.

00:35:49.490 --> 00:35:54.450
I know classmates who have been
trying to switch either industries,

00:35:54.450 --> 00:35:56.820
geographies, or function, and

00:35:56.820 --> 00:36:01.290
I think there are opportunities available
through various different resources.

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:05.800
One is the Career Management Services,
the CMC as we call it here, and

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:08.780
they organize workshops,
special workshops for

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:12.930
international students on how does
American style of interviewing happens.

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:17.150
They have company networking nights,
where companies from

00:36:17.150 --> 00:36:20.690
all sorts of different industries come and
you can interact with them.

00:36:20.690 --> 00:36:24.340
And they also have on campus
a recruitment process.

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:27.834
In terms of networking,
it goes beyond just the CMC.

00:36:27.834 --> 00:36:32.330
And as alluded to there
are brown bag lunches, and

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:38.042
what we call BBLs hosted by different
companies all throughout the year.

00:36:38.042 --> 00:36:41.501
And that's a good way to one
appreciate what these companies do and

00:36:41.501 --> 00:36:44.897
also get to know the professionals there,
be in touch with them,

00:36:44.897 --> 00:36:49.390
learn from them over there, and
not just during the recruitment season.

00:36:49.390 --> 00:36:53.109
And in terms of
professional opportunities,

00:36:53.109 --> 00:36:58.446
there are a lot of activities that
go on from interviewing skills,

00:36:58.446 --> 00:37:04.856
to building your resume, to actually
figuring out what career is best for you.

00:37:04.856 --> 00:37:08.529
So I remember some of the workshops in
the beginning of the year which were super

00:37:08.529 --> 00:37:11.870
helpful in terms of just thinking
through what sort of career would help

00:37:11.870 --> 00:37:13.721
you find the most meaning in your life.

00:37:13.721 --> 00:37:14.650
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:37:14.650 --> 00:37:19.244
&gt;&gt; So I think it's a really vast
ocean of opportunities, and

00:37:19.244 --> 00:37:25.160
it ranges from very philosophical
to very tactical opportunities.

00:37:25.160 --> 00:37:27.080
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
plus one to everything he said.

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:32.133
Apart from that, a little bit of
detail about how the networking and

00:37:32.133 --> 00:37:34.269
recruitment happens here.

00:37:34.269 --> 00:37:39.221
So there are two ways, like alluded to,
one is the on-campus recruitment

00:37:39.221 --> 00:37:43.982
way which kind of gets by January and
mid February and you have.

00:37:43.982 --> 00:37:45.041
&gt;&gt; So the traditional track, yeah.

00:37:45.041 --> 00:37:49.000
&gt;&gt; Yeah it's the very traditional kind
of companies that come on campus.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:54.320
Then there is a networking
based job search which happens

00:37:54.320 --> 00:37:57.630
later in the winter quarter and
through your spring quarter.

00:37:57.630 --> 00:38:02.380
One unique thing about Stanford is that
lot of people, especially for internships,

00:38:02.380 --> 00:38:07.570
really want to pursue new things, so they
tend to prefer network-based job search.

00:38:07.570 --> 00:38:09.980
And what exactly helps
network-based job search?

00:38:09.980 --> 00:38:13.330
There are two different avenues that
I can think of, one is the clubs.

00:38:13.330 --> 00:38:17.900
A lot of these companies approach clubs
and the clubs conduct the BVOs that

00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:22.320
was talking about, that's a great way to
network and apply to these companies.

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:25.420
And then CMC also has a lot
of alumni information,

00:38:25.420 --> 00:38:28.360
you just need to figure out what
companies you're interested in.

00:38:28.360 --> 00:38:31.570
There are career advisers that will help
you figure out how to approach the company

00:38:31.570 --> 00:38:34.910
and who are the right people that you
need to contact in those companies.

00:38:34.910 --> 00:38:38.970
And the third, sometimes use
resources are professors I would say,

00:38:38.970 --> 00:38:42.700
a lot of our professors
are practitioners in industry.

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:46.640
They all either operate companies
currently, or they have operated

00:38:46.640 --> 00:38:50.190
companies in the past so they are also
very well connected in the industries.

00:38:50.190 --> 00:38:54.420
So in first year, slightly difficult

00:38:54.420 --> 00:38:58.030
to get access to those professors because
a lot of them teach in the second year.

00:38:58.030 --> 00:39:01.840
But still it can be done
because nobody is going to deny

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:05.410
helping you if you reach out to them,
you just need to know who to reach out to.

00:39:05.410 --> 00:39:10.380
So professors are kind of slightly
underrated, but a very useful resource.

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:11.780
&gt;&gt; I want to touch a little bit on,

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:16.560
there are of course a lot of resources as
you mentioned, and also there is the AAP,

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:20.370
the academic adjustment period,
which begins in your first quarter.

00:39:20.370 --> 00:39:25.262
So how did that affect your, or tell
us a little bit about that experience,

00:39:25.262 --> 00:39:30.080
sort of that time that you
can not have to worry so

00:39:30.080 --> 00:39:33.230
much about the career trajectory.

00:39:34.930 --> 00:39:38.910
&gt;&gt; Sure, so academic adjustment
period is the first six weeks

00:39:40.040 --> 00:39:41.815
when you get in the GSB.

00:39:41.815 --> 00:39:46.743
And I think it’s a really important
period because when you come in here most

00:39:46.743 --> 00:39:50.650
of you would be moving to the US Most
likely for the first time,

00:39:50.650 --> 00:39:51.770
you will be living in the US.

00:39:51.770 --> 00:39:56.528
You'll be meeting a set of new friends,
new classmates,

00:39:56.528 --> 00:40:01.300
getting to settle in a new
educational environment.

00:40:01.300 --> 00:40:04.450
So this basically six weeks provide

00:40:04.450 --> 00:40:09.510
a buffer where you are not exposed to any
career activities or any club activities.

00:40:09.510 --> 00:40:14.090
And you can just focus on
settling into the system,

00:40:14.090 --> 00:40:18.220
get to know your classmates well, spend
some time socially, and things like that.

00:40:18.220 --> 00:40:20.030
So I think for me, personally,

00:40:20.030 --> 00:40:23.380
AB was a great time to just make
myself comfortable in the system

00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:27.360
before the floodgates open, and
you start including or joining the clubs.

00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:28.249
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you.

00:40:28.249 --> 00:40:29.605
[LAUGH] Thanks.

00:40:29.605 --> 00:40:32.850
All right, well we're coming
to the close of our webinar.

00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:37.810
But before we go, do either of you
have any final words of advice?

00:40:37.810 --> 00:40:40.166
I'll start with you, Shusmita.

00:40:40.166 --> 00:40:44.635
&gt;&gt; I think when I applied to GSB,
I had a very

00:40:44.635 --> 00:40:49.540
one-dimensional approach to it,
like I want to switch my careers.

00:40:49.540 --> 00:40:54.050
But I think a GSB experience
is a lot more than that.

00:40:54.050 --> 00:40:58.315
It's a lot about knowing yourself better,
and becoming a better person.

00:40:58.315 --> 00:41:01.005
I'm not just throwing some
corny lines out there.

00:41:01.005 --> 00:41:01.795
It's really true.

00:41:01.795 --> 00:41:06.985
There are so many experiences like talk
at GP Lee and a bunch of other courses.

00:41:06.985 --> 00:41:11.955
Just the community around you which
really helps you understand yourself and

00:41:11.955 --> 00:41:13.325
be a better version of yourself.

00:41:14.925 --> 00:41:18.849
And I'm totally having a lot of fun.

00:41:18.849 --> 00:41:22.731
I would do it all over again if I have to.

00:41:22.731 --> 00:41:24.136
&gt;&gt; Yeah, thank you.

00:41:25.263 --> 00:41:26.786
&gt;&gt; So for me, it's very similar.

00:41:26.786 --> 00:41:31.391
One little addition I had here is
basically technical skills and article

00:41:31.391 --> 00:41:36.340
skills are simply hygiene as you think of
leadership roles and interpersonal skills.

00:41:36.340 --> 00:41:40.060
And how you lead teams is
basically what differentiates you

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:42.820
as you grow up the corporate ladder.

00:41:42.820 --> 00:41:46.410
And I think GSB just provides you that
lab where you can experiment with

00:41:46.410 --> 00:41:49.960
different styles of your leadership or
how you interact with people.

00:41:49.960 --> 00:41:54.680
And I think that journey is
such invaluable resource that

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:57.428
you will be thankful for
it for the rest of your life.

00:41:57.428 --> 00:42:01.568
&gt;&gt; That's great, great advice,
both of you, thank you.

00:42:01.568 --> 00:42:04.733
Thank you both for
joining us, and thanks for

00:42:04.733 --> 00:42:07.907
all of you out there
who were participating.

00:42:07.907 --> 00:42:12.315
You can learn more about the Stanford MBA
program on our website and

00:42:12.315 --> 00:42:14.686
by watching our videos on YouTube.

00:42:14.686 --> 00:42:20.396
[MUSIC]

